WEBVTT

1
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:21.855
Here, in the vastness of the cosmos,
sits a solar system.

2
00:00:23.649 --> 00:00:28.570
If we look closely enough,
just past Mars and next to the moon,

3
00:00:29.238 --> 00:00:33.075
we can see a small blue
planet floating in space.

4
00:00:33.784 --> 00:00:34.868
This is Earth.

5
00:00:35.911 --> 00:00:36.912
Here we are.

6
00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:47.089
Okay. Close your eyes.

7
00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:51.885
I'm holding a baby,
I'm not closing my eyes.

8
00:00:52.302 --> 00:00:53.387
Right.

9
00:00:53.470 --> 00:00:55.472
Sorry. Okay. Come in.

10
00:00:59.893 --> 00:01:00.978
Okay.

11
00:01:02.312 --> 00:01:04.147
And watch this.

12
00:01:06.441 --> 00:01:07.860
No way!

13
00:01:08.694 --> 00:01:10.028
Did you make this?

14
00:01:10.112 --> 00:01:12.114
Yes. Yes, I did.

15
00:01:12.197 --> 00:01:13.198
And your mom helped.

16
00:01:13.282 --> 00:01:16.118
It's all so sweet. Really.

17
00:01:18.287 --> 00:01:20.080
We used to be a family of two.

18
00:01:21.373 --> 00:01:22.583
Now look at us.

19
00:01:32.426 --> 00:01:33.635
Are you all right?

20
00:01:34.678 --> 00:01:35.888
It's so much bigger.

21
00:01:36.597 --> 00:01:37.598
What?

22
00:01:38.682 --> 00:01:39.683
The Earth.

23
00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:42.895
It's so much bigger than that,

24
00:01:43.604 --> 00:01:45.856
and a million times more complicated.

25
00:01:48.192 --> 00:01:52.404
If Finn is gonna navigate this planet,
he'll need to know, well, everything.

26
00:01:52.487 --> 00:01:55.115
Which means we have to
teach him everything we know

27
00:01:55.199 --> 00:01:56.992
and everything we don't know.

28
00:01:57.659 --> 00:01:59.119
But how do we do that?

29
00:01:59.203 --> 00:02:00.954
I don't even know where to start.

30
00:02:01.038 --> 00:02:02.664
I'm so lost.

31
00:02:02.748 --> 00:02:04.082
All right. Breathe...

32
00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:05.876
and sit down.

33
00:02:08.252 --> 00:02:09.420
Look,

34
00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:13.425
this is new for both of us,
but we'll figure it out.

35
00:02:14.426 --> 00:02:17.262
It's like my mom always said,
"If you're feeling lost,

36
00:02:17.346 --> 00:02:21.975
just start with what you know,
then take it one thing at a time."

37
00:02:22.518 --> 00:02:25.604
Suddenly,
I feel like I don't know very much at all.

38
00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:27.814
Come here.

39
00:02:28.524 --> 00:02:30.192
You know at least one thing:

40
00:02:31.068 --> 00:02:33.570
We have a perfect little baby.

41
00:02:34.613 --> 00:02:36.031
Yeah. I guess so.

42
00:02:39.326 --> 00:02:42.996
Why don't you get some sleep.
We need a little father-son time.

43
00:02:43.413 --> 00:02:44.414
Okay.

44
00:02:52.422 --> 00:02:54.174
Hello.

45
00:02:54.842 --> 00:02:55.843
I'm your dad.

46
00:02:56.593 --> 00:02:58.428
We met earlier at the hospital.

47
00:03:00.764 --> 00:03:02.391
Let me give you the tour.

48
00:03:04.434 --> 00:03:05.853
Here we are.

49
00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:07.479
Planet Earth.

50
00:03:09.815 --> 00:03:11.275
Yeah, it's pretty big out there.

51
00:03:11.358 --> 00:03:16.071
Maybe, Maybe let's start with
the easy stuff, like this apartment.

52
00:03:17.155 --> 00:03:19.575
It's where you live with me and your mom.

53
00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:22.870
What do you think?

54
00:03:24.162 --> 00:03:25.372
It's nice, isn't it?

55
00:03:27.624 --> 00:03:29.042
And here's where we eat.

56
00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:31.920
Here's where we keep the food cold.

57
00:03:32.462 --> 00:03:34.256
And here's where we keep the food hot.

58
00:03:34.339 --> 00:03:35.340
Yeah.

59
00:03:36.258 --> 00:03:39.011
Someday I'll make
breakfast for you with this pan.

60
00:03:39.094 --> 00:03:41.096
Daddy's very good at pancakes.

61
00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:42.931
Here's where we do bath time.

62
00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:47.269
And here's where we'll do
bath time when you're older.

63
00:03:48.103 --> 00:03:51.023
Here's where you'll sleep and wake up.

64
00:03:51.481 --> 00:03:53.150
And dream.

65
00:03:55.861 --> 00:03:57.279
These are called books.

66
00:03:57.362 --> 00:03:58.363
They're wonderful.

67
00:03:59.114 --> 00:04:01.742
You'll wanna come back
here when you can read.

68
00:04:04.536 --> 00:04:05.996
I know you can't speak yet,

69
00:04:06.079 --> 00:04:08.957
and I'm sure your head
must be filled with questions,

70
00:04:09.041 --> 00:04:10.042
but be patient.

71
00:04:10.125 --> 00:04:12.669
You'll learn how to use words soon enough,

72
00:04:13.587 --> 00:04:15.547
and then you'll use them forever.

73
00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:18.341
Now then, let's see.

74
00:04:20.135 --> 00:04:22.679
Eight planets revolve around the sun.

75
00:04:22.763 --> 00:04:26.391
Saturn's rings are made of dust,
rock and ice.

76
00:04:26.475 --> 00:04:31.188
The world's tallest volcano
is Mauna Kea in Hawaii.

77
00:04:31.271 --> 00:04:35.776
The octopus is considered the
most intelligent of all invertebrates.

78
00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:40.822
The blue whale is the largest
animal known to have ever existed.

79
00:04:41.573 --> 00:04:45.244
Polaris is also known as the North Star.

80
00:04:45.327 --> 00:04:47.538
The incandescent lightbulb uses far more...

81
00:05:08.934 --> 00:05:10.352
Finn, turn it off.

82
00:05:11.395 --> 00:05:12.312
What?

83
00:05:13.272 --> 00:05:14.273
Off!

84
00:05:14.690 --> 00:05:16.358
But I wanna see how it works!

85
00:05:17.317 --> 00:05:18.402
- What?
- What?

86
00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:20.654
I wanna see how...

87
00:05:21.697 --> 00:05:23.490
I wanna see how it works.

88
00:05:24.741 --> 00:05:26.201
Time for breakfast, hon.

89
00:05:30.747 --> 00:05:32.040
Great.

90
00:05:32.124 --> 00:05:35.169
Give it time. You only checked yesterday.

91
00:05:35.252 --> 00:05:37.671
So, this clock is really cool.

92
00:05:37.754 --> 00:05:40.424
There's a spring,
and it presses against this...

93
00:05:40.507 --> 00:05:42.134
Incredibly loud alarm clock?

94
00:05:42.217 --> 00:05:44.511
Dad! No, this other thing.

95
00:05:44.595 --> 00:05:48.557
And when the hand reaches the right place,
it sets off the spring and...

96
00:05:50.934 --> 00:05:52.728
I think we got it, thanks.

97
00:05:53.854 --> 00:05:58.233
But here's a question for you: Why
do we have clocks in the first place?

98
00:05:58.317 --> 00:05:59.359
To tell time.

99
00:06:00.277 --> 00:06:02.279
But why do we have to tell time?

100
00:06:02.362 --> 00:06:06.450
So... so people can...

101
00:06:07.159 --> 00:06:08.243
I don't know.

102
00:06:08.911 --> 00:06:10.329
Because time is precious.

103
00:06:11.121 --> 00:06:16.418
So maybe the better we organize our time
into hours, into minutes, into seconds,

104
00:06:16.919 --> 00:06:18.962
the better we can spend our time.

105
00:06:19.796 --> 00:06:23.592
Look, it's great that you want to
know how the alarm clock works,

106
00:06:24.176 --> 00:06:28.138
but sometimes you need to step
back and wonder why it works.

107
00:06:28.764 --> 00:06:32.100
Try to see the big picture.

108
00:06:32.935 --> 00:06:35.020
How do you both know everything?

109
00:06:35.687 --> 00:06:38.106
Earth pancakes?

110
00:06:38.190 --> 00:06:39.191
For Earth Day.

111
00:06:40.984 --> 00:06:44.363
Not too bad at all.

112
00:06:45.822 --> 00:06:48.534
The countries are all in the right places,
yes?

113
00:06:48.992 --> 00:06:52.663
Well, I wouldn't chart a
course by them or anything.

114
00:06:54.748 --> 00:06:56.333
Did you pack the lunch for the park?

115
00:06:56.416 --> 00:06:57.668
We're going to the park?

116
00:06:58.210 --> 00:06:59.545
For Earth Day, remember?

117
00:07:00.170 --> 00:07:02.923
Isn't the park near The
Museum of Everything?

118
00:07:03.006 --> 00:07:04.216
Yeah, I think it is.

119
00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:05.801
Okay.

120
00:07:05.884 --> 00:07:07.553
And yes. Lunch is packed,

121
00:07:07.636 --> 00:07:10.597
and the forecast has changed,
so it looks like sunny skies.

122
00:07:10.681 --> 00:07:12.391
Can we have a family meeting?

123
00:07:12.891 --> 00:07:14.309
Yes, we can.

124
00:07:14.393 --> 00:07:15.394
Great.

125
00:07:16.603 --> 00:07:19.439
I propose that we go to The
Museum of Everything today.

126
00:07:19.523 --> 00:07:22.484
It's Earth Day,
and Earth is part of everything.

127
00:07:23.068 --> 00:07:25.487
So the museum would be perfect.

128
00:07:26.154 --> 00:07:30.868
Plus, there's a rocketry exhibit, which
probably has my top five favorite rockets,

129
00:07:30.951 --> 00:07:32.619
and plenty of other space stuff,

130
00:07:32.703 --> 00:07:37.916
like the Milky Way and Sputnik,
and, you know, space things.

131
00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:40.961
You know that nobody loves
museums more than I do,

132
00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:43.297
but today is Earth Day.

133
00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:46.842
We should be enjoying the planet outside.

134
00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:54.600
And also, the museum gift
shop probably has telescopes.

135
00:07:55.142 --> 00:07:59.438
And if I had one, I could see
my top five favorite constellations.

136
00:07:59.521 --> 00:08:01.732
Which are Scorpius, Draco...

137
00:08:01.815 --> 00:08:04.026
Buddy, we're going outside.

138
00:08:04.109 --> 00:08:05.944
But the museum is outside.

139
00:08:06.028 --> 00:08:07.821
It's outside this apartment building.

140
00:08:07.905 --> 00:08:09.114
That counts.

141
00:08:09.198 --> 00:08:10.741
No, it doesn't.

142
00:08:11.617 --> 00:08:13.702
That's a clever argument, but no.

143
00:08:13.785 --> 00:08:16.121
But the spaceships...

144
00:08:16.205 --> 00:08:19.041
You can come back to outer
space when we get home.

145
00:08:19.124 --> 00:08:20.292
- Hello.
- Hi.

146
00:08:20.375 --> 00:08:21.877
- Morning.
- Hi.

147
00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:24.254
Sumiko, that's a cool outfit.

148
00:08:24.338 --> 00:08:25.464
Thank you.

149
00:08:25.547 --> 00:08:28.550
And more geographically
accurate than your pancakes.

150
00:08:29.009 --> 00:08:30.093
Long story.

151
00:08:30.844 --> 00:08:32.554
So, where are you headed today?

152
00:08:32.638 --> 00:08:34.806
You know, just a little trip to the park.

153
00:08:34.890 --> 00:08:36.433
Us too, but a bit later.

154
00:08:36.517 --> 00:08:37.934
Maybe we'll see you there.

155
00:08:38.602 --> 00:08:39.602
- Bye.
- Bye-bye.

156
00:08:39.686 --> 00:08:40.687
Bye.

157
00:08:42.813 --> 00:08:43.815
Right then.

158
00:08:54.952 --> 00:08:56.203
Hold up there, Finn.

159
00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:07.256
Look. The Museum of Everything.

160
00:09:07.339 --> 00:09:09.299
Can we go? Please?

161
00:09:10.008 --> 00:09:11.426
Come on now. Not today.

162
00:09:15.055 --> 00:09:16.181
Come on.

163
00:09:16.723 --> 00:09:19.393
There's a lot of fresh air
out there we need to breathe,

164
00:09:19.476 --> 00:09:21.061
and plenty of grass to picnic in

165
00:09:21.144 --> 00:09:27.025
and birds and squirrels, and
butterflies to watch and trees to climb.

166
00:09:27.568 --> 00:09:28.569
Yay.

167
00:09:38.787 --> 00:09:41.415
Okay. So, I just want to say,

168
00:09:41.498 --> 00:09:44.042
it's not too late to go to
The Museum of Everything.

169
00:09:44.126 --> 00:09:45.169
Seriously?

170
00:09:45.252 --> 00:09:48.589
Do you know what we can learn
about in The Museum of Everything?

171
00:09:48.964 --> 00:09:50.924
Earth. It's perfect.

172
00:09:53.302 --> 00:09:56.138
I think the park is telling
you to stay outside.

173
00:09:57.097 --> 00:09:58.557
Agreed. Let's go.

174
00:09:59.391 --> 00:10:00.976
I can't win.

175
00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:18.660
Hey, Dad, how much faster
does a rocket go than our bikes?

176
00:10:19.244 --> 00:10:23.498
Well, we're probably going,
I don't know, 15 kilometers an hour

177
00:10:23.582 --> 00:10:28.212
while a rocket goes faster
than that. So I would...

178
00:10:28.295 --> 00:10:31.048
You know what,
we can figure this all out later.

179
00:10:31.507 --> 00:10:34.676
For today,
let's just keep our thoughts here on Earth.

180
00:10:39.723 --> 00:10:42.309
Keep her steady, keep her... Yes. Great.

181
00:10:54.863 --> 00:10:57.866
Hey, Finn. Sure you don't want to join us?

182
00:10:57.950 --> 00:11:00.202
Yeah, come on, buddy,
the wind is just right.

183
00:11:00.285 --> 00:11:01.537
It's easy, it's just...

184
00:11:10.128 --> 00:11:11.171
Nice.

185
00:11:11.255 --> 00:11:12.589
There you go. Look at that.

186
00:11:12.673 --> 00:11:14.925
I mean,
it's better than sitting on a stump, right?

187
00:11:15.008 --> 00:11:16.552
But I like sitting.

188
00:11:16.635 --> 00:11:18.262
Then I have an idea.

189
00:11:24.601 --> 00:11:26.812
Maybe I don't like sitting.

190
00:11:32.609 --> 00:11:33.610
Hello, fish.

191
00:11:45.080 --> 00:11:46.164
Hello!

192
00:12:00.971 --> 00:12:02.139
No.

193
00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:10.355
"You are here."

194
00:12:23.702 --> 00:12:25.704
This is great.

195
00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:33.212
What you thinking about,
Finn? You seem distracted.

196
00:12:33.295 --> 00:12:35.714
I found a really cool
bike trail on the map.

197
00:12:35.797 --> 00:12:39.176
And after lunch,
we should all go for a ride on our bikes.

198
00:12:40.052 --> 00:12:42.179
And along the way, we can look at nature

199
00:12:42.262 --> 00:12:45.682
and think about how great the
Earth is because it's Earth Day.

200
00:12:45.766 --> 00:12:46.975
Good idea, right?

201
00:12:47.518 --> 00:12:48.519
Okay.

202
00:12:52.856 --> 00:12:54.066
Cool trail.

203
00:12:54.691 --> 00:12:56.276
Look at those trees!

204
00:12:56.735 --> 00:12:59.947
I know. Nature is so beautiful.

205
00:13:06.578 --> 00:13:08.330
The Museum of Everything.

206
00:13:08.413 --> 00:13:11.625
I'm so surprised.

207
00:13:11.959 --> 00:13:13.794
Yeah, what a surprise.

208
00:13:14.294 --> 00:13:19.842
Since we're here, I don't know,
maybe we could go to the museum?

209
00:13:20.467 --> 00:13:22.261
He totally tricked us.

210
00:13:22.344 --> 00:13:25.973
You gotta hand it to him,
the kid has persistence.

211
00:13:26.056 --> 00:13:27.558
And ingenuity.

212
00:13:27.641 --> 00:13:29.768
Part of me is really proud.

213
00:13:29.852 --> 00:13:32.437
Well, I think he won this round.

214
00:13:32.896 --> 00:13:34.356
Okay, shall we?

215
00:13:34.815 --> 00:13:35.816
Yes!

216
00:13:55.794 --> 00:13:56.795
Come on!

217
00:14:07.472 --> 00:14:08.473
Hi.

218
00:14:08.932 --> 00:14:09.933
All right, bud.

219
00:14:10.392 --> 00:14:13.395
So you got your way.
We're here at the museum.

220
00:14:13.478 --> 00:14:16.773
But if you're gonna be here,
you should really learn something.

221
00:14:17.983 --> 00:14:21.069
Let's see,
let's see. Where should we start?

222
00:14:23.322 --> 00:14:24.323
Finn.

223
00:14:24.823 --> 00:14:26.033
Over here!

224
00:14:37.794 --> 00:14:43.592
Ankylosaurus, Carnotaurus, Diplodocus,
Apatosaurus and Spinosaurus.

225
00:14:44.301 --> 00:14:46.386
"The Diplodocus is related to..."

226
00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:49.431
What? He was just here.

227
00:14:49.515 --> 00:14:50.516
Come on!

228
00:14:58.774 --> 00:14:59.983
What's that?

229
00:15:02.194 --> 00:15:05.405
That is you on the inside.

230
00:15:06.698 --> 00:15:08.700
Here's my brain for thinking,

231
00:15:09.451 --> 00:15:12.120
my lungs for breathing,

232
00:15:12.996 --> 00:15:14.623
my heart pumps my blood,

233
00:15:15.791 --> 00:15:17.960
and my bones hold it all together.

234
00:15:20.379 --> 00:15:23.131
And there's a lot to look after,
so look after it.

235
00:15:23.215 --> 00:15:24.842
Most bits don't grow back.

236
00:15:27.219 --> 00:15:32.307
What about my hair and my
toenails and my fingernails?

237
00:15:32.391 --> 00:15:33.475
You're right.

238
00:15:33.976 --> 00:15:35.394
Those do grow back.

239
00:15:35.811 --> 00:15:37.020
Very clever.

240
00:15:40.524 --> 00:15:43.068
Hang on. I was enjoying that.

241
00:15:52.494 --> 00:15:57.583
All right! Liftoff in five,
four, three, two...

242
00:15:58.500 --> 00:15:59.543
one?

243
00:16:02.963 --> 00:16:06.341
Hey, I'm so sorry, but we're full up,

244
00:16:06.842 --> 00:16:10.053
and this exhibit is now
closed for the rest of the day.

245
00:16:11.763 --> 00:16:12.764
No!

246
00:16:13.599 --> 00:16:14.808
Never mind, Finn.

247
00:16:16.351 --> 00:16:18.478
Maybe there's something else to see.

248
00:16:40.042 --> 00:16:41.502
Let's check this out.

249
00:16:47.841 --> 00:16:49.134
This looks cool.

250
00:16:54.973 --> 00:16:56.225
It's so big.

251
00:17:15.327 --> 00:17:16.537
Is it broken?

252
00:17:17.663 --> 00:17:19.873
I think it might be out of service.

253
00:17:20.624 --> 00:17:21.708
Wait a second.

254
00:17:30.092 --> 00:17:31.301
Hello?

255
00:17:33.971 --> 00:17:35.097
Okay.

256
00:17:37.724 --> 00:17:38.767
Well, hello.

257
00:17:39.685 --> 00:17:40.894
Hang on.

258
00:17:43.814 --> 00:17:45.649
Welcome to this planet.

259
00:17:45.732 --> 00:17:47.526
We call it Earth.

260
00:17:47.609 --> 00:17:51.238
It is the big globe floating
in space on which we live.

261
00:17:53.156 --> 00:17:56.743
We're glad you found us,
as space is very big.

262
00:17:59.746 --> 00:18:05.252
There is much to see and do here on Earth,
so let's get started with a quick tour.

263
00:18:06.253 --> 00:18:08.922
The planet is basically made up of...

264
00:18:09.548 --> 00:18:11.383
What's...

265
00:18:11.466 --> 00:18:12.926
Was that supposed to happen?

266
00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:14.720
I guess the voice is broken.

267
00:18:16.889 --> 00:18:18.098
Can you do it?

268
00:18:19.308 --> 00:18:20.517
Sure.

269
00:18:21.185 --> 00:18:25.898
The Earth is basically made
of two parts: land and sea.

270
00:18:26.440 --> 00:18:27.441
And...

271
00:18:27.524 --> 00:18:30.277
First, let's talk about the land.

272
00:18:30.819 --> 00:18:32.362
We know lots about it.

273
00:18:32.863 --> 00:18:35.073
The desert is dry and hot.

274
00:18:36.033 --> 00:18:37.576
The plains are flat.

275
00:18:38.368 --> 00:18:39.745
The hills are bumpy.

276
00:18:40.412 --> 00:18:43.498
The tops of volcanoes can be very hot...

277
00:18:46.793 --> 00:18:49.796
...while the tops of
mountains can be very cold.

278
00:18:53.050 --> 00:18:58.013
And now we're above the sea,
which is full of wonderful things.

279
00:18:58.096 --> 00:19:00.182
It goes nearly seven miles deep.

280
00:19:00.599 --> 00:19:01.808
Is that true?

281
00:19:01.892 --> 00:19:03.060
Yeah. The...

282
00:19:03.143 --> 00:19:04.353
Pretty sure.

283
00:19:04.436 --> 00:19:07.648
The "Mariana Trench" in the Pacific.

284
00:19:07.731 --> 00:19:09.399
It says it right here. Look.

285
00:19:10.692 --> 00:19:12.444
Do you see that?

286
00:20:02.035 --> 00:20:03.161
No way!

287
00:20:21.597 --> 00:20:22.848
Look at that.

288
00:20:25.142 --> 00:20:26.894
Okay.

289
00:20:27.603 --> 00:20:31.023
The sea has creatures
of all shapes and sizes.

290
00:20:31.106 --> 00:20:36.612
From the tiny seahorse to the...

291
00:20:36.695 --> 00:20:38.322
Giant octopus?

292
00:20:38.405 --> 00:20:40.240
Yes! Octopus. Good.

293
00:20:52.586 --> 00:20:54.463
The bottom of the sea is very dark,

294
00:20:54.546 --> 00:21:00.636
but luckily many kinds of fish
produce their own light so they can see.

295
00:21:02.095 --> 00:21:03.472
It looks awesome!

296
00:21:27.704 --> 00:21:28.705
Okay.

297
00:21:29.623 --> 00:21:31.542
There's also the sky.

298
00:21:32.084 --> 00:21:34.586
Though, that can get pretty complicated.

299
00:21:34.670 --> 00:21:36.338
This looks like...

300
00:21:38.131 --> 00:21:39.716
Earth's atmosphere.

301
00:21:42.094 --> 00:21:43.554
And...

302
00:21:45.138 --> 00:21:48.225
And this Stratos-thingy.

303
00:21:50.435 --> 00:21:54.731
The atmosphere is the layer of gases,
which we call air,

304
00:21:54.815 --> 00:21:56.650
that surrounds the Earth.

305
00:21:56.733 --> 00:21:58.151
Thank goodness. She's back.

306
00:21:58.235 --> 00:21:59.528
You were good.

307
00:21:59.611 --> 00:22:00.612
Thanks.

308
00:22:01.572 --> 00:22:06.410
The tiniest change in the atmosphere
can have a huge effect on our weather

309
00:22:06.493 --> 00:22:12.124
and on our lives, producing rain,
wind, snow, fog and more.

310
00:22:14.835 --> 00:22:17.337
When we take a bigger step back,

311
00:22:17.421 --> 00:22:22.009
we can see that the moon
spins around us here on Earth,

312
00:22:22.092 --> 00:22:24.845
while the Earth spins around the sun.

313
00:22:25.971 --> 00:22:30.976
And in the very far distance,
almost six trillion miles away,

314
00:22:31.643 --> 00:22:33.645
we can see stars,

315
00:22:33.729 --> 00:22:38.859
bright, burning balls of gas
that dot the sky on a clear night.

316
00:22:41.945 --> 00:22:46.241
But closer to home,
down here on our planet,

317
00:22:46.325 --> 00:22:47.743
there are people.

318
00:22:48.243 --> 00:22:52.664
People come in many shapes,
sizes and colors.

319
00:22:53.540 --> 00:22:57.628
We may all look different,
act different, sound different,

320
00:22:58.086 --> 00:23:04.885
but don't be fooled, we are all people,
and we all share the Earth.

321
00:23:08.639 --> 00:23:10.599
There are animals too.

322
00:23:10.682 --> 00:23:14.520
They come in even more shapes,
sizes and colors.

323
00:23:14.603 --> 00:23:19.566
They can't speak to us, though
that's no reason not to be nice to them.

324
00:23:19.650 --> 00:23:20.901
There you go.

325
00:23:22.653 --> 00:23:26.740
Things can sometimes
move slowly here on Earth.

326
00:23:27.658 --> 00:23:32.412
More often though, they move quickly,
so use your time well.

327
00:23:33.413 --> 00:23:35.457
Because time is precious.

328
00:23:42.130 --> 00:23:46.260
Though we have come a long way,
there is plenty left for you to do.

329
00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:49.680
You will figure lots of
things out for yourself.

330
00:23:50.097 --> 00:23:53.392
Just remember to leave
notes for everybody else.

331
00:23:54.184 --> 00:23:57.062
Well, that is planet Earth.

332
00:23:57.604 --> 00:24:01.775
Make sure you look after it,
as it's all we've got.

333
00:24:05.529 --> 00:24:07.573
That was epic.

334
00:24:08.031 --> 00:24:09.324
Did you like it, hon?

335
00:24:12.202 --> 00:24:15.747
For the first time in his life,
he's speechless.

336
00:24:16.290 --> 00:24:18.000
Can we have a family meeting?

337
00:24:18.876 --> 00:24:19.877
Sure.

338
00:24:20.919 --> 00:24:24.715
I propose we go back to the park,
like, right now.

339
00:24:25.716 --> 00:24:26.800
Really?

340
00:24:26.884 --> 00:24:29.469
Yeah. I mean, the museum is really cool,

341
00:24:29.553 --> 00:24:32.890
but we should be at the park,
especially today.

342
00:24:35.142 --> 00:24:36.476
And off he goes again.

343
00:24:38.604 --> 00:24:39.813
Hello, trees.

344
00:24:43.066 --> 00:24:44.276
Hello, lake.

345
00:24:46.278 --> 00:24:47.487
Hello, fish.

346
00:24:50.532 --> 00:24:51.909
Hello, birds.

347
00:24:54.786 --> 00:24:56.121
Hey, butterflies.

348
00:25:07.633 --> 00:25:11.261
You know, if you count the rings,
you can find out how old it is.

349
00:25:11.720 --> 00:25:12.888
Yeah!

350
00:25:13.889 --> 00:25:20.187
One, two, three, four,
five, six, seven, eight...

351
00:25:21.438 --> 00:25:25.025
...forty-one, 142, 143.

352
00:25:26.902 --> 00:25:29.029
A hundred forty-three rings.

353
00:25:29.112 --> 00:25:33.742
That means this tree was 143 years old.

354
00:25:33.825 --> 00:25:36.328
That's older than Grandma!

355
00:25:37.079 --> 00:25:39.748
Yes. That's older than Grandma's grandma.

356
00:25:42.793 --> 00:25:44.711
The clouds are beautiful today.

357
00:25:46.839 --> 00:25:50.050
Is that one an octopus riding a dolphin?

358
00:25:50.467 --> 00:25:53.804
No, it's more like a car full of balloons.

359
00:25:54.221 --> 00:25:55.556
Very nice.

360
00:25:57.015 --> 00:25:59.434
Now it's just a bowl of mashed potatoes.

361
00:26:00.185 --> 00:26:01.895
So, what do you see up there?

362
00:26:04.606 --> 00:26:05.732
Hey, Finn.

363
00:26:07.484 --> 00:26:08.610
You okay?

364
00:26:10.195 --> 00:26:14.783
So to this bug, I must look really big.

365
00:26:15.158 --> 00:26:21.123
Yeah. The way you look to that
insect is probably similar to the way...

366
00:26:21.206 --> 00:26:23.458
that building looks to you.

367
00:26:26.962 --> 00:26:29.006
It's so big.

368
00:26:33.635 --> 00:26:36.805
That's really big.

369
00:26:44.188 --> 00:26:47.149
Well, it was fun while it lasted.

370
00:26:47.858 --> 00:26:49.401
Come on. Let's get home.

371
00:27:25.938 --> 00:27:28.232
You've been pretty quiet, Finn. What's up?

372
00:27:28.315 --> 00:27:31.735
I... Well, you know the bug at the park?

373
00:27:33.737 --> 00:27:35.155
What were you gonna say, hon?

374
00:27:35.864 --> 00:27:37.241
Great...

375
00:27:37.324 --> 00:27:38.659
What's happening?

376
00:27:39.117 --> 00:27:41.912
Looks like the power's
out. Must've been the storm.

377
00:27:42.287 --> 00:27:43.956
It's nothing to worry about.

378
00:27:45.582 --> 00:27:46.708
I have an idea.

379
00:27:56.134 --> 00:27:59.012
We were thinking we
might go upstairs on the roof.

380
00:27:59.096 --> 00:28:02.850
With the power out,
the stars will be really bright.

381
00:28:03.392 --> 00:28:04.852
Yeah, okay.

382
00:28:05.310 --> 00:28:07.437
You two... You go ahead.

383
00:28:07.521 --> 00:28:08.730
I'll be up soon.

384
00:28:29.042 --> 00:28:31.336
It's pretty.

385
00:28:32.921 --> 00:28:34.131
Yes, it is.

386
00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:40.470
So... the telescope you wanted?

387
00:28:40.971 --> 00:28:43.348
We were saving it for your birthday,

388
00:28:44.183 --> 00:28:48.604
but we're giving it to you right now.

389
00:28:49.229 --> 00:28:50.480
No way!

390
00:28:50.564 --> 00:28:53.984
Yes way! It's a present for Earth Day.

391
00:28:54.067 --> 00:28:56.445
Thank you.

392
00:29:01.950 --> 00:29:04.912
All right. I'll leave you
for some father-son time.

393
00:29:05.287 --> 00:29:07.039
I'm gonna go check on the neighbors.

394
00:29:07.122 --> 00:29:09.249
Right, then.

395
00:29:18.425 --> 00:29:19.635
All right.

396
00:29:20.135 --> 00:29:24.973
I'm pretty sure this one is Scorpius,
your favorite constellation.

397
00:29:29.770 --> 00:29:31.647
All right, what's going on?

398
00:29:31.730 --> 00:29:34.608
You've been quiet ever
since we were at the park.

399
00:29:37.152 --> 00:29:40.280
Well, you know how Mom always
says to look at the big picture?

400
00:29:40.739 --> 00:29:41.740
Sure.

401
00:29:41.823 --> 00:29:44.660
Well, at the museum,
and then later at the park,

402
00:29:44.743 --> 00:29:47.829
I felt like I was starting to
see the big picture. And...

403
00:29:48.247 --> 00:29:49.623
It was too big?

404
00:29:50.666 --> 00:29:51.667
Yeah.

405
00:29:52.334 --> 00:29:55.254
And I always wanted to know everything,
you know?

406
00:29:55.337 --> 00:29:56.547
I know.

407
00:29:57.047 --> 00:29:58.465
And now, it's like...

408
00:29:58.549 --> 00:30:02.886
It's like, the world is so huge
that no matter how much I learn,

409
00:30:03.262 --> 00:30:05.013
it'll never be enough.

410
00:30:05.556 --> 00:30:09.601
I feel like how that bug at the park
must've felt when it was looking up at me.

411
00:30:09.685 --> 00:30:12.104
Well, it sounds like you feel...

412
00:30:12.187 --> 00:30:13.188
Lost.

413
00:30:14.565 --> 00:30:16.233
Hey, come here.

414
00:30:17.943 --> 00:30:21.113
Finn, I completely understand.

415
00:30:21.196 --> 00:30:24.741
It can be scary to think
about how big the world is.

416
00:30:24.825 --> 00:30:27.119
But sometimes,
if you look at it the right way,

417
00:30:27.202 --> 00:30:29.955
the world can be much
smaller than you think.

418
00:30:30.664 --> 00:30:32.666
You know what Mom once told me?

419
00:30:32.749 --> 00:30:36.503
"When you're lost,
just start with what you know."

420
00:30:37.796 --> 00:30:39.756
You know where the Big Dipper is?

421
00:30:40.299 --> 00:30:41.717
Sure. Right there.

422
00:30:42.176 --> 00:30:43.218
Exactly.

423
00:30:43.302 --> 00:30:47.472
Well, those two stars at the end there,
they point directly at...

424
00:30:47.556 --> 00:30:48.599
The North Star.

425
00:30:48.682 --> 00:30:50.934
Right. Polaris. So...

426
00:30:51.810 --> 00:30:55.230
So that means you can
always find north in the night sky.

427
00:30:55.314 --> 00:30:57.691
It's always there, wherever you are.

428
00:30:57.774 --> 00:31:00.360
And if you know where north is,

429
00:31:00.444 --> 00:31:04.865
you can start to find your way
home and you won't be so lost.

430
00:31:06.033 --> 00:31:08.076
Dad, does everyone get lost?

431
00:31:08.493 --> 00:31:12.539
Yes, they do. At some point,
every single person gets lost.

432
00:31:12.623 --> 00:31:15.292
Then we should show them
all where the North Star is.

433
00:31:15.375 --> 00:31:16.960
Yeah.

434
00:31:17.044 --> 00:31:20.172
Yeah, I mean, that's a good start,
but it's a little more complicated.

435
00:31:20.631 --> 00:31:25.969
I'd say, just treat everyone
with kindness and respect.

436
00:31:26.053 --> 00:31:29.306
And then you can help
people feel a little less lost.

437
00:31:30.349 --> 00:31:34.853
And listen, if you ever need
to know anything else, just ask.

438
00:31:34.937 --> 00:31:37.147
I won't be far away.
And when I'm not there,

439
00:31:37.231 --> 00:31:39.191
you can always ask someone else.

440
00:31:43.070 --> 00:31:44.488
- Hi.
- Bonsoir.

441
00:31:44.571 --> 00:31:45.489
- Hey, there.
- Hola.

442
00:31:45.572 --> 00:31:46.448
- Konnichiwa.
- Privet.

443
00:31:46.532 --> 00:31:47.449
- Hi.
- Shalom.

444
00:31:47.533 --> 00:31:49.451
You're never alone on Earth.

445
00:31:56.959 --> 00:31:57.960
Look.

446
00:32:37.457 --> 00:32:38.458
That's better.

447
00:32:41.920 --> 00:32:44.131
Is that your telescope?

448
00:32:45.007 --> 00:32:47.885
Yeah. You wanna take a look?

449
00:32:47.968 --> 00:32:48.969
Yeah!

450
00:32:56.852 --> 00:32:58.979
So that's Scorpius. It looks like a...

451
00:32:59.062 --> 00:33:00.189
Scorpion!

452
00:33:00.272 --> 00:33:03.317
Exactly! It's my favorite thing in space.

453
00:33:04.026 --> 00:33:05.360
Actually, wait.

454
00:33:05.444 --> 00:33:06.820
No, it isn't.

455
00:33:06.904 --> 00:33:08.155
What is?

456
00:33:08.238 --> 00:33:11.366
Earth. Earth is my favorite thing in space.

457
00:33:37.935 --> 00:33:40.437
Here we are on Earth.

458
00:33:41.605 --> 00:33:46.360
When you travel far enough away from Earth,
one thing becomes clear:

459
00:33:46.818 --> 00:33:52.282
This planet, alone in the cosmos,
is the place where people live.

460
00:33:53.033 --> 00:33:54.743
And live we do.

461
00:33:55.494 --> 00:33:58.872
Life on Earth is a wonderful thing.

462
00:34:01.041 --> 00:34:05.295
It looks big,
but there are lots of us on here.

463
00:34:05.379 --> 00:34:10.592
Seven and a half billion at last count,
with more showing up every day.

464
00:34:11.844 --> 00:34:15.138
Even so,
there is still enough for everyone,

465
00:34:15.764 --> 00:34:17.766
if we all share a little.

466
00:34:18.433 --> 00:34:20.768
So please be kind.

467
00:34:22.688 --> 00:34:27.359
Of course, there is so much more for
you to learn about living on planet Earth.

468
00:34:28.025 --> 00:34:32.906
But for now, these are the
things we think you need to know.
